  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\u2217$ | $0.35$ | $\u2217$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\u2217$ | $0.25$ | $?$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(X=2),$ rounded to two decimal places?",
  "worked_solution": "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=1) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1) \\\\ 0.25 & =P(X=2)\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ P(X=2) & =\\frac{0.25}{0.6} \\\\  & \u22480.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
